15:18:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really honest and important point—and I think it’s the real heart of your discomfort. When something is made in a lab, hasn’t been in the human food supply for long, and we’re told it’s safe, it can feel like a giant, uncontrolled experiment... and you’re the subject. That unease is completely rational. So let’s sit with that question honestly: **How can anyone say an Impossible Burger is totally fine when we don’t have 30-year studies on it?** ### The short, uncomfortable truth is: They can’t say it’s “totally fine” with 100% certainty. There’s no 30-year randomized controlled trial following people who eat Impossible Burgers daily. In that strict sense, you’re right—there’s a gap in long-term epidemiological data for this specific collection of ingredients. That uncertainty is real. However, the reason experts and regulators are generally comfortable with it isn’t because they have a crystal ball. It’s because they’re using a **weight-of-evidence** approach that draws on a few things: 1. **The individual ingredients aren’t new.** - Soy protein isolate has been widely consumed since the 1960s. - Methylcellulose has been used in foods for decades. - Coconut oil and sunflower oil have been part of human diets longer than any of us have been alive. - The only truly novel ingredient is **soy leghemoglobin (heme)** produced by fermentation. But it’s chemically identical to the heme in your own blood and in plants. It went through a battery of short- and medium-term animal feeding studies (up to 28 days in rats, and target species studies) that looked for toxicity, organ damage, and allergenicity. No red flags were found. That’s not the same as a human lifetime study, but it’s the same standard used for many other food additives. 2. **We can make educated predictions from the structure and short-term data.** Because the proteins, fats, and carbohydrates in these burgers are almost identical to those in foods we already know, scientists aren’t starting from zero. They look at digestibility, allergen potential, and metabolic pathways. If something hasn’t shown any signal of harm in rigorous short- and medium-term animal studies, and the chemistry gives no reason to suspect a long-term ticking time bomb, the reasonable assumption is that it’s safe—while keeping a watchful eye. 3. **We’re comparing it to a known long-term risk.** Here’s the ironic twist: we actually *do* have 20- and 30-year data on red meat and processed meat consumption. The evidence is strong enough that the World Health Organization classifies processed meat as a Group 1 carcinogen (causes cancer in humans) and red meat as Group 2A (probably causes cancer). I’m not saying a beef burger is equivalent to smoking, but we do have long-term evidence that regular consumption stresses the body, promotes cardiovascular disease, and increases colorectal cancer risk. So when someone says “we don’t know what Impossible Burgers do in 30 years,” a reasonable counterpoint is: **we do know what the thing it’s replacing does**, and it isn’t good. For a person who’s going to eat a burger either way, betting on the one without the known long-term carcinogens may be the safer gamble, even with some residual uncertainty. 4. **Regulatory bodies are conservative.** The FDA didn’t just take the company’s word on the heme ingredient. They conducted multiple rounds of review, asked for additional safety studies, and ultimately issued a “no questions” letter (for GRAS status) based on the data. That’s not a guarantee of eternal safety, but it’s a rigorous, science-based stamp of confidence that no foreseeable harm exists. ### So where does that leave you? It leaves you exactly where you are: cautious. And that’s a perfectly defensible position. If it freaks you out, you don’t have to eat it. There is absolutely no nutritional need to consume an Impossible Burger—you can get all the protein, fiber, and nutrients you need from whole plants like lentils, chickpeas, and tofu that have centuries of safe use behind them. But if the question is “How can scientists claim it’s healthy?”—the answer isn’t that they’re ignoring the unknown. It’s that they’re weighing the known harms of the alternative against the low likelihood of a hidden long-term danger from well-studied components. For a population that eats a lot of burgers, the math likely favors the plant-based option, even with its lab-made origins. Your fear doesn’t make you irrational—it makes you appropriately cautious about putting something novel into your body. The best defense is exactly what you’re doing: asking hard questions, listening to that instinct, and choosing whole foods when you can. If you ever do eat an Impossible Burger, it will be with your eyes open, knowing what we do and don’t know. And that’s a much better place than blind trust.
